---
import SourcePanel from '../components/SourcePanel.astro';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ASSETS } from '../data/assets';
import { SOURCES } from '../data/sources';

const title = 'Grave Seasons Killer, Decisions and Replay Guide';
const description =
  'A spoiler-safe Grave Seasons guide to the killer candidate pool, autosave decisions, resident observation, romance risk, and replay planning.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Killer, Decisions & Replay', url: '/killer-decisions-and-replay/' },
];
const sources = [SOURCES.steam, SOURCES.steamDevlog, SOURCES.blumhouse];
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} image={ASSETS.townNight.src}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="section-kicker">Spoiler-safe investigation guide</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl font-black leading-tight text-bone md:text-5xl">
      Killer, Decisions & Replay Planning
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fog">
      Grave Seasons is built to make a second investigation meaningful. This page explains the official replay structure and gives you a way to record choices without revealing a killer identity that the pre-release material has not confirmed.
    </p>

    <figure class="mt-8">
      <img src={ASSETS.townNight.src} alt={ASSETS.townNight.alt} width={ASSETS.townNight.width} height={ASSETS.townNight.height} class="rounded-md border border-white/10" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption class="mt-2 text-xs text-fog">{ASSETS.townNight.credit}</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-page mt-8">
      <h2>The confirmed replay structure</h2>
      <p>
        Steam says every playthrough chooses a supernatural serial killer from a pool of candidates. The selected killer can change the campaign's reasoning, kill style, and targets. The same listing also says decisions are checked and autosaved, so you should treat important choices as persistent until the finished game proves that a branch can be undone.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not a promise that every outcome is permanently missable. It is a practical rule for a game whose central mystery changes from run to run: write down what happened instead of reloading on instinct.
      </p>

      <h2>What the story objectives are confirmed to be</h2>
      <p>
        Individual quest names have not been published, but the official Steam and Blumhouse descriptions name the
        kinds of objectives the investigation is built around. Use them as the headings for your own quest log:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Protect potential victims</strong> from the supernatural serial killer within one year.</li>
        <li><strong>Discover methods that ward off monsters</strong>, which ties the occult-crafting side of the game to survival.</li>
        <li><strong>Unravel the truth behind the supernatural murders.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Uncover the secrets Ashenridge residents keep hidden.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Unlock storylines through both death and survival</strong> - the store text says characters can have stories tied to either outcome.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because decisions are described as having positive and negative consequences, record each objective with the
        season, day, location, and resident state when it started, and keep killer identity and deaths behind spoiler
        labels when you share notes. The <a href="/occult-items-victim-protection/">occult items and victim protection</a>
        page covers the warding side in more detail.
      </p>

      <h2>Keep a spoiler-safe suspect ledger</h2>
      <p>Use one row per resident with only observations in the first pass:</p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Field</th><th>Record</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Identity</td><td>Name, home, work, and any confirmed relationship.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Routine</td><td>Where and when you saw the resident, including season.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Direct clue, second-hand statement, or only a suspicious coincidence.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Decision</td><td>What you chose and the immediate result.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Confidence</td><td>Confirmed fact, observed event, interpretation, or unknown.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Keep interpretation in its own column. A resident being absent from a location is an observation; calling that absence proof of guilt is a theory. This distinction will make a second run much easier to compare.
      </p>

      <h2>Separate an observation from a suspect theory</h2>
      <p>For each observation, record the run identifier, game version, season, day, time, resident, location, exact action or dialogue, and your interpretation in separate fields. These are suggested notebook fields, not confirmed game interface options. A screenshot of Yuto at a shop establishes one scene; it does not establish a daily schedule or a killer identity.</p>
      <p>When comparing two runs, keep their notes separate. A changed target might reflect the selected killer, a prior survival decision, or another condition; one difference cannot identify the cause. The store confirms a candidate pool but does not publish its size, selection probabilities, or whether consecutive runs can select the same killer. An exhaustive universal culprit checklist would therefore promise more than the available evidence supports.</p>
      <h2>Log decisions before the result</h2>
      <p>
        Before selecting a major dialogue option, note the date, location, people present, and the question you are trying to answer. After the scene, add the result. Because the game advertises checked and autosaved decisions, this record is more useful than a series of reloads that hides the branch logic.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Mark whether the choice changed a relationship, a clue, a location, or only the dialogue.</li>
        <li>Use a separate tag for romance decisions; do not assume romance and investigation outcomes are independent.</li>
        <li>Capture the season and time of day. The 24-hour clock and changing seasons can alter what you can observe next.</li>
        <li>When a case closes, list the unanswered questions before starting another route.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Romance without losing the mystery</h2>
      <p>
        The official store page lists 12 potential romanceable people and warns that a few may be the killer. That makes relationship progress a source of character context, but also a spoiler boundary. Keep a romance notebook separate from the public suspect summary, and label any guide screenshot that reveals a route or outcome.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pre-release material does not confirm which romance options belong to which killer scenario, how affection is earned, or whether a relationship can be repaired after a decision. The <a href="/romance-guide/">romance guide</a> tracks confirmed scope and will need launch-build route testing.
      </p>

      <h2>Compare runs instead of chasing one perfect run</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Finish a first run while recording observations, not spoilers.</li>
        <li>Copy the farm, season, and resident baseline into a new sheet.</li>
        <li>Compare only changed clues, targets, routines, and decision results.</li>
        <li>Test one hypothesis at a time so a new killer scenario is not confused with a changed dialogue choice.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The useful replay question is not "what is the universal best route?" It is "which evidence and decisions changed this case?" The official design intentionally makes the answer variable.
      </p>

      <h2>Known unknowns</h2>
      <p>
        Killer identities, candidate names, exact target schedules, murder scenes, route locks, and the number of distinct outcomes are not published in the official sources checked for this page. Do not use a fan theory as a confirmed spoiler until the release build or a first-party source supports it.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the time pressure behind this system, read <a href="/tips/">Grave Seasons Tips</a>. For the town's official scope, see <a href="/characters/">Characters</a> and the <a href="/killer-decisions-and-replay/">investigation notes guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SourcePanel sources={sources} />
  </article>
</Layout>

